<BaseLayout
  title="Resources | Masahiro Sadamura"
  description="Vignettes, NLP code, and reference lists shared for research transparency in legal psychology."
>
  <h1>Research Resources</h1>

  <p>
    To support research reproducibility and contribute to the academic
    community, I share vignette materials, analysis code, and reference
    lists where possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    When using these resources, please cite the source
    (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" rel="license noopener" target="_blank">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    <small>This page is being expanded over time; the items below will be released in step with OSF preregistration milestones.</small>
  </p>

  <h2>Vignettes</h2>
  <h3>Sentencing Vignettes (in preparation)</h3>
  <p>Vignettes used in sentencing-judgment studies 1–3 will be released as their preregistration status permits.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Study 1</strong>: Sentencing-decision vignettes manipulating welfare-oriented information about the defendant (developmental history, current support context, future support availability).</li>
    <li><strong>Study 2</strong>: Vignettes derived from anonymized and condensed actual sentencing decisions.</li>
    <li><strong>Study 3</strong>: Pilot vignettes for cases involving elderly offenders with welfare-oriented support contexts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Public release will follow OSF preregistration milestones.</p>

  <h2>Analysis Code</h2>
  <h3>Sentencing-text NLP pipeline (in preparation)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Language: Python</li>
    <li>Key libraries: scikit-learn, MeCab, SHAP, transformers</li>
    <li>Use case: structuring sentencing texts (from court websites, Daiichi Hoki, Westlaw), extracting sentencing factors, and estimating defendant-feature contributions to sentencing outcomes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mediation and path analysis in R</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Libraries: lavaan, tidyverse, semTools, semPlot</li>
    <li>Use case: testing focal concerns theory in sentencing decisions, estimating mediated effects, and conducting between-group comparisons.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Planned location</h3>
  <p>GitHub (to be set up; URL will be added here).</p>

  <h2>Reference Lists</h2>
  <h3>Welfare-oriented support and sentencing (in preparation)</h3>
  <p>A curated subset of the reference database, useful for researchers in adjacent fields.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Development of focal concerns theory</li>
    <li>Conceptual history of redeemability</li>
    <li>Cooperation between criminal justice and welfare in cases involving elderly and disabled offenders</li>
    <li>Comparative studies of lay-judge and professional-judge sentencing</li>
    <li>Methodology of vignette experiments</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Empirical reviews of sentencing decisions (in preparation)</h3>
  <p>Reviews of major domestic and international sentencing studies (with future meta-analytic intent).</p>

  <h2>Educational Materials</h2>
  <h3>Statistical materials (in preparation)</h3>
  <p>Supporting materials prepared in the course of research, organized for undergraduate and graduate students.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing and analyzing vignette experiments (factorial designs, sample size, ANCOVA)</li>
    <li>Mediation and conditional mediation analysis (R implementation analogous to PROCESS)</li>
    <li>Interpreting machine learning models (using SHAP, with caveats)</li>
    <li>Bridging qualitative and quantitative research (mixed methods)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sharing Policy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All resources are released under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" rel="license noopener" target="_blank">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a> (<strong>non-commercial use only; redistribution requires the same license</strong>).</li>
    <li>No prior permission is required, but please cite the source when used in publications or presentations.</li>
    <li>Bug reports and improvement suggestions: GitHub Issues (to be set up).</li>
  </ul>
